<header><p class="enc-kicker">State</p><h1 class="enc-title">Biafra</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1967">1967</a>–<a href="/years/1970">1970</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Republic of Biafra</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Biafra, officially the Republic of Biafra, was a partially recognised state in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-africa">West Africa</a> that declared independence from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nigeria">Nigeria</a> and existed from 1967 to 1970. It covered the former Eastern Region of Nigeria, predominantly inhabited by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/igbo-people">Igbo</a>, and was established on 30 May 1967 by the region's governor, Chukwuemeka Odumegwu Ojukwu, after military coups and the 1966 anti-Igbo pogrom. The Nigerian attack of 6 July 1967 began the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nigerian-civil-war">Nigerian Civil War</a>; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gabon">Gabon</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/haiti">Haiti</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivory-coast">Côte d'Ivoire</a>, Tanzania and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zambia">Zambia</a> recognised Biafra. Almost two million Biafran civilians died, most from starvation under Nigeria's <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naval-blockade">blockade</a>, before Ojukwu fled to the Ivory Coast and Philip Effiong oversaw the surrender.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The name Biafra appears on early modern maps as Biafar, Biafara or Biafares, applied by European travellers to a region around present-day West Cameroon. The state of 1967 arose from the politics of independent <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nigeria">Nigeria</a>, which became independent from the United Kingdom in 1960 with borders that did not reflect earlier ethnic, cultural, religious or political boundaries. It was divided along ethnic lines, with a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hausa">Hausa</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fula">Fulani</a> majority in the north, a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yoruba">Yoruba</a> majority in the west and an <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/igbo-people">Igbo</a> majority in the east. Riots had broken out at Jos in 1945 and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kano">Kano</a> in 1953, and a crisis in the Western Region in 1964 spread violence across the country.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Ojukwu decreed secession after consulting community leaders from across the Eastern Region, citing the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/igbo-people">Igbos</a> killed in the violence after the coup. The region's oil also fed the conflict. Biafra had about 3,000 soldiers at the start and 30,000 at the end, fewer than <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nigeria">Nigeria</a>, but fought in its homeland. The federal government attacked on 6 July 1967. The Biafrans occupied areas of the mid-Western Region in August 1967, but with British, American and Soviet support Nigeria regained the land by October 1967. Gowon's &quot;final offensive&quot; of September 1968 was at first held and later broke through.</p><p>In 1968 images of starving Biafran children reached Western media, bringing in aid organisations and the Biafran airlift; Médecins Sans Frontières originated among French doctors who had witnessed the suffering. The Bank of Biafra issued its own Biafran pound from 28 January 1968. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, Portugal, Israel, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-africa">South Africa</a> and Rhodesia gave de facto support, while Lyndon Johnson's United States stayed officially neutral. About two million Biafran civilians died.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>Ojukwu went into exile as the federal army approached the capital, and Philip Effiong oversaw the surrender. In 2000 the Nigerian president Olusegun Obasanjo commuted the dismissal of those who had fought for Biafra to retirement, saying that justice must be tempered with mercy. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/igbo-people">Igbo</a> nationalism remained strong. The Movement for the Actualization of the Sovereign State of Biafra appeared in 1999 as a nonviolent group, and the Indigenous People of Biafra was founded in 2012 by Nnamdi Kanu.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Biafra” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Captives from the Igbo hinterland were carried in large numbers to the ports of the Bight of <b>Biafra</b>, such as Bonny and Calabar, and the Aro, with their oracle at Arochukwu and their chain of trading settlements, built a new kind of power that ritual sanctions could not…</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-nri">Kingdom of Nri · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>…750,000 dead, when eleven states claimed the right to leave; Nigeria’s federation was torn by the secession of <b>Biafra</b> in 1967–1970, in which hundreds of thousands died, most of them from famine; and the communist federations broke apart along the boundaries of their republics, Yugoslavia in a series…</blockquote><a href="/government/federation">Federation · Weaknesses</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Biafra</b>'s acting leader Philip Effiong announced the end of the secessionist state's resistance.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/january-12">12 January in history</a></li><li><blockquote>Colonel Odumegwu Ojukwu proclaimed the Republic of <b>Biafra</b> in Nigeria's south-east.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/may-30">30 May in history</a></li><li><blockquote>Two military coups in 1966 ended the first republic, and in May 1967 Emeka Ojukwu declared the Eastern Region the Republic of <b>Biafra</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/nigeria">Nigeria</a></li></ul></section>
